\section{What is asked of the data}{
\code{x} is the recall and \code{y} the precision, both fractions of one, in any
order: the average precision is measured over the points sorted by
recall. Several classifiers on one chart are several groups, split by
\code{colour}, \code{linetype} or \code{group} as a multi-series line is, and each is
announced by its group's name.
}

\section{Curves maidr reads without a declaration}{
\code{ggplot2::autoplot()} of a \code{\link[yardstick:pr_curve]{yardstick::pr_curve()}} draws a \code{geom_path()}
that maps \code{recall} against \code{precision}, and is read as a precision-recall
curve as it stands -- as is any \code{geom_line()} or \code{geom_path()} of columns
named \code{recall} and \code{precision}. Such a curve carries neither the
thresholds nor the share of positives into the plot, so no threshold is
announced, the average precision is measured from the points, and the
points are not read against a baseline; \code{maidr_pr_curve()} is how an
author supplies them.

A Base R \code{plot(recall, precision, type = "l")} (or \code{type = "s"}) and a
lattice \code{xyplot(precision ~ recall, type = "l")} are read the same way
when their axes are titled \code{Recall} and \code{Precision} -- which both take
from the variables' names unless \code{xlab} and \code{ylab} say otherwise -- and
every value is a fraction of one.
}

